const COURSE_15_QUIZZES_DEF = {
  c15q_l1:{ title:'Quiz: Requirements and plan', qs:[
    { q:'Why does a company network separate employees, servers and guests into groups?',
      opts:['because each group pays for its own separate internet line and the provider must therefore precisely meter every bit of their data use','because they have different trust levels, and separating them shrinks the blast radius of an incident','because otherwise their devices couldn’t connect to the same switch and cable'],
      correct:1, why:'The groups have different trust levels (server > employee > guest). Separating them shrinks the blast radius: an infected guest then can’t reach the servers directly.' },
    { q:'What does ‘blast radius’ mean in network design?',
      opts:['the maximum physical distance a Wi-Fi signal reaches from the access point','the speed at which the whole network recovers from a power outage before all of its devices come back online again','how far the damage from one incident (infection, attack) spreads before something stops it'],
      correct:2, why:'The blast radius is the reach of damage. Good segmentation shrinks it: one hijacked element should take down at most itself and a few neighbors, not the whole company.' },
    { q:'How, per the course, do you correctly approach network design?',
      opts:['first buy as many devices as possible and only then work out what each is good for','top-down: first decide who may talk to whom (the plan), only then with what you do it (VLAN, router, firewall)','bottom-up: first wire up all the cables and addresses across the whole company and only write the plan once the network is already done'],
      correct:1, why:'Design is top-down: first WHAT you want (who may talk to whom), then WITH WHAT. A plan on paper before the cables saves later rewiring and holes.' }
  ]},
  c15q_l2:{ title:'Quiz: Segmentation into VLANs', qs:[
    { q:'What do you gain by splitting a flat network into VLANs for employees, servers and guests?',
      opts:['separate networks without pulling new cables: a device in one VLAN can’t reach another on its own','faster internet, because each VLAN gets its own connection to the provider','automatic data backup, because a VLAN quietly copies files between all the different groups of devices for you'],
      correct:0, why:'A VLAN splits one physical switch into several separate networks without new cables. A security boundary appears: neither broadcast nor an attack spills out of the VLAN.' },
    { q:'Why is ‘client isolation’ enabled on guest Wi-Fi?',
      opts:['so guests can’t download large files and slow down the employees’ connection','so guests can’t see each other and one infected guest can’t infect the next one’s laptop','so guests must first register with a name and a password at the company reception desk'],
      correct:1, why:'Client isolation ensures two guests on the same Wi-Fi can’t see each other. The VLAN separates them from the company, client isolation from each other – a guest gets the bare minimum.' },
    { q:'After the VLAN split, an employee can’t even reach the file server. What does this mean?',
      opts:['that the VLAN must be misconfigured and you have to delete the whole thing and restart the segmentation again from scratch','that it’s expected: a VLAN is a wall, crossed in a controlled way only by the router (inter-VLAN routing)','that the file server is broken and needs a reboot to start responding again'],
      correct:1, why:'That is exactly the wall it’s about. A VLAN separates even groups that should talk. The router connects them in a controlled way in lesson 3 – hence a single path leads through it.' }
  ]},
  c15q_l3:{ title:'Quiz: Router and addresses', qs:[
    { q:'What is the gateway (default gateway) each subnet gets for?',
      opts:['it’s the address a device sends all traffic heading outside its own subnet to','it’s the fastest server in the subnet, through which updates are downloaded for other devices','it’s a backup address used only when the device’s main address stops working'],
      correct:0, why:'The gateway is the address (usually .1, on the router) a device sends everything outside its subnet to. The router then forwards the packet to the target VLAN or out to the internet.' },
    { q:'Why does a company need NAT to reach the internet?',
      opts:['to encrypt all internet traffic so the provider can’t eavesdrop on it along the way','because internal private addresses aren’t valid on the public internet; the router rewrites them to one public one','because without NAT the whole internet connection would be much slower and web pages would take longer to load than usual'],
      correct:1, why:'Internal addresses (10.0.x.x) are private and invalid on the internet. NAT/PAT rewrites them to one public address on the way out and returns replies by port.' },
    { q:'How does an employee in VLAN 10 reach the file server in VLAN 20?',
      opts:['directly, because devices in the same company always talk regardless of which VLAN they’re in','not at all, a direct connection between two separate VLANs is technically impossible and data must be moved by hand','it sends the packet to its gateway (the router), which recognizes the target subnet and forwards it into VLAN 20'],
      correct:2, why:'The target is in another subnet, so the computer sends the packet to the gateway = the router. By its table it sees the target is in VLAN 20 and forwards it there. That is inter-VLAN routing.' }
  ]},
  c15q_l4:{ title:'Quiz: Firewall and zones', qs:[
    { q:'Why is the web server placed in the DMZ, not in the internal network (inside)?',
      opts:['because in the DMZ it has a much faster connection to the internet and the pages load noticeably sooner for visitors','because it must be reachable from the internet; if it fell in inside, the attacker has a foot straight in the company','because for technical reasons no servers can be placed in inside, only employee computers'],
      correct:1, why:'The web must be reachable from outside and is thus most exposed. In the DMZ, after hijacking the web the attacker is stuck in a separate zone and can’t reach inside (and sensitive data).' },
    { q:'What does the ‘default-deny’ rule on a firewall mean?',
      opts:['that all traffic is blocked and the network stops working until the admin manually disables the firewall','that whatever no rule explicitly allows is automatically forbidden (the last rule at the bottom)','that every new connection is allowed first and the firewall blocks it only once it causes a problem'],
      correct:1, why:'Default-deny: whatever is not explicitly allowed is forbidden. You allow a narrow list of the necessary and everything else drops by itself – so you can’t forget to block something.' },
    { q:'The DMZ web is allowed inbound from the internet on 443. Why also forbid it from initiating connections into inside?',
      opts:['to save firewall performance, since checking DMZ-to-inside connections is CPU-heavy','so a hijacked web can’t hop onward to the file server and spread the attack deeper into the company','because the web needs no connection anywhere and the rule would just needlessly fill the rule table'],
      correct:1, why:'The DMZ must not initiate connections into inside precisely so a hijacked web can’t hop to the file server. That is the whole point of isolating the server in the DMZ.' }
  ]},
  c15q_l5:{ title:'Quiz: Test and harden', qs:[
    { q:'What is most important to verify when testing a finished network, per the course?',
      opts:['only that the download speed matches what the company pays its internet provider','not only what SHOULD pass but especially what MUST NOT – negative tests (guest to servers) reveal holes in the rules','it’s enough that the web loads in a browser; if the web works, all the other paths need no separate verification'],
      correct:1, why:'Test both: positive paths (employee to files, web on 443) and negative (guest to servers = must drop). Negative tests reveal holes that ‘looks fine’ misses.' },
    { q:'What is the topology audit for, compared with testing individual paths?',
      opts:['it speeds up the network by merging unused VLANs and subnets into one, freeing address space','it walks the network as a whole and flags weak spots and over-broad rules a single-path test misses','it automatically fixes every found problem, so after running it you need to change nothing yourself'],
      correct:1, why:'The audit lifts the view above single tests: it walks the network as a whole and finds forgotten broad rules or needlessly exposed services. You then do the fix (hardening) yourself.' },
    { q:'Why is network security described as a loop, not a one-off task?',
      opts:['because the firewall must be manually rebooted every hour, or it eventually stops filtering traffic','because the network changes (a new server, a new app) and after every change you must retest and re-audit','because it’s just a marketing term with no practical meaning; in reality you set the network up once and it’s done'],
      correct:1, why:'A network lives and changes. After every change (a new server, app or rule) you must retest paths and re-audit so no new hole appears. Hence a loop.' }
  ]}
};
